          heading: "Why data design comes first",
          narration: "Hello again, everyone. Last week we built the two halves of an app, React in the browser and Express on the server, and wrestled CORS into submission. But right now our app has the memory of a goldfish. Restart the server and everything's gone. So this week: databases. And let me tell you why I take this topic so seriously. Your web servers can be restarted, replaced or multiplied whenever you like. We saw that with horizontal scaling. The database is different. It's the long-lived source of truth, and it holds the one thing you can't regenerate: your users' data. That's why decisions about storing data outlast almost every other decision in a project. A bad schema leaks into your API, your frontend and your reports, and it's painful to change once real data exists. I've spent entire weekends migrating data out of a schema someone designed in an afternoon. Today we compare the main families: relational databases queried with SQL, document databases from the NoSQL family, and graph databases. None of them is best in general. The skill is asking how your app reads and writes its data, and picking the model that fits.",
