          heading: "Designing a REST API with JSON",
          narration: "Let's design the API properly. REST is a style for HTTP APIs built around resources. You name each resource with a URL made of nouns, like slash api slash tasks for the collection and slash api slash tasks slash 12 for one task, and you let the HTTP method express the action, just like we mapped last week. Data travels as JSON, JavaScript Object Notation: a text format of objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans and null. When you send JSON, set the Content-Type header to application slash json, so the server knows how to read it. Now, a few habits that separate a pleasant API from one your teammates curse under their breath. Be consistent with naming and response shapes across every endpoint. If one endpoint says user id in camel case and another uses an underscore, someone will lose an afternoon to it. Return the created or updated resource, so the client doesn't need a second request. Use query parameters for filtering, sorting and paging, like asking for completed tasks only. And pick one predictable error format, say an object with a message and a list of field problems, so the frontend can show errors the same way everywhere.",
