          heading: "Status codes as a contract",
          narration: "Status codes are how your API tells clients, and your tests, what actually happened, so precision matters. In the success range: 200 is general success, 201 means something was created, 204 means success with nothing to send back. In the client error range, 400 Bad Request means the input was malformed or failed validation. Then the two everyone mixes up. 401 Unauthorized, despite the name, really means unauthenticated. The request has no valid credentials, so the server doesn't know who you are. Logging in might fix it. 403 Forbidden means the server knows exactly who you are, and the answer is still no. Logging in again won't help. Think of a nightclub. 401 is the bouncer saying, show me some ID. 403 is the bouncer reading your ID and saying, sorry, you're not on the list. 404 means the resource doesn't exist, and 409 Conflict suits things like registering an email that's already taken. Finally, 500 means the server itself failed, and it should never be caused by ordinary bad input. So if a test calls a protected endpoint without logging in, the right expectation is 401. Not a crash. Not an empty success.",
