          heading: "Calling the API from React",
          narration: "In the browser, you'll usually call your API with fetch. It returns a promise, so you await it, then await response dot json to read the body. Simple enough. But there's one behaviour that catches almost everyone, and it caught me during a live demo once, in front of investors. Fetch only rejects its promise when the request couldn't be completed at all: a network failure, a DNS problem, a response blocked by CORS. If the server replies with a 404 or a 500, that's still a completed HTTP exchange, so fetch resolves perfectly happily. My demo dashboard cheerfully tried to render an error message as if it were a list of customers. Not a great look. So your code must check response dot ok, which is true only for status codes from 200 to 299, or look at response dot status, and handle the error itself. In a component, a robust pattern tracks three bits of state: the data, a loading flag, and an error message. Show a spinner while loading, a friendly message on error, and the data otherwise. Wrap that in a small helper, and every component handles failure the same way.",
