          heading: "Configuration and secrets",
          narration: "Your app needs configuration that changes between environments: the database connection string, the port, the allowed CORS origin, API keys for other services. The Twelve-Factor App methodology, a widely followed set of guidelines for cloud apps, says configuration belongs in environment variables, not in code. Two reasons. First, security. A connection string usually contains a password, and anything committed to version control should be treated as leaked, because repositories get shared, forked and made public, and Git history keeps old commits. Now, confession time. Early in my career, I committed an API key to a public repository. I deleted it ten minutes later. Too late. Someone's automated scanner had already found it, and I got a very educational bill. Second reason, flexibility: the same code runs on your laptop against a local database and in production against a managed one, just by changing variables. In Node, you read them from process dot env. During development, a dot env file loaded by a library like dotenv is handy, but that file must go in your gitignore. Commit a dot env dot example with the variable names and fake values, so teammates know what to set.",
