          narration: "Hi everyone, and welcome to week five, which is secretly my favourite week. Last week we wired up a full app and I kept waving my hands about 401s and 403s. Today we earn them. Two words sound alike but mean different things, and mixing them up causes genuine security bugs. Authentication answers: who are you? It's proving identity, with a password, a passkey, a code from an app, or a sign-in through another provider. Authorisation answers: what are you allowed to do? It happens after authentication, and it checks permissions, like whether this user may see this record or call an admin route. The status codes follow exactly the same split. No valid credentials? The server responds 401, because it can't establish who you are. A known identity, but no permission? 403 Forbidden. So when a logged-in student requests an admin-only route, the answer is 403. Authentication worked fine. Authorisation said no. Remember last week's nightclub bouncer: ID check first, guest list second. Keep those two steps separate in your code too. One piece establishes the identity, and later checks decide what that identity may do.",
