          narration: "Hello again, everyone. Last week we learnt ASP.NET Core on its own: the pipeline, dependency injection, Razor and EF Core. This week we find out why it's so popular inside organisations. It plugs neatly into the wider Microsoft ecosystem. Lots of universities, governments and businesses already run on Microsoft 365, with Outlook mail, Teams, OneDrive and SharePoint, and they manage staff and student accounts centrally. Azure is Microsoft's cloud platform, offering hosting, databases, storage and a great many other services. And the glue holding it together is identity: one directory that knows every user and every application, and issues the tokens that let them talk to each other. When your app joins that world, users sign in with the work or university account they already have, your app can read their calendar or files with permission, and it can run on Azure without storing passwords anywhere. I once worked on an internal tool with its own separate login, and the number one support request, by a mile, was forgotten passwords. Plugging into the organisation's identity made that whole category of tickets disappear overnight. Today: Entra ID for identity, Microsoft Graph for data, and Azure App Service and Azure SQL for hosting.",
