          heading: "Secrets in pipelines",
          narration: "A pipeline needs credentials: an AWS access key, an SSH private key, a database URL. Where those live is one of the biggest security decisions you'll make. They must never be committed to the repository. Not in a dot env file, not in the README, and not hard-coded into the workflow YAML. Repositories get copied, forked and sometimes made public, and Git history keeps old commits even after you delete the line. And yes, here comes the story again, because it's the most expensive lesson I ever learned. When I committed that API key years ago, an automated scanner found it before I'd even noticed my mistake. Deleting it didn't help. So instead, GitHub gives you encrypted secrets, stored at repository, environment or organisation level. In a workflow, you reference a secret through the secrets context, written as a dollar sign and double braces around secrets dot followed by its name. GitHub injects the value at runtime and masks it in logs. Environments add protection, like requiring approval before production secrets are used. Better still, where supported, use OpenID Connect between GitHub and AWS, so the workflow assumes an IAM role with short-lived credentials, and there's no long-lived key to leak at all.",
