          heading: "SSH into Ubuntu servers",
          narration: "To deploy to an EC2 instance running Ubuntu, you connect with SSH, the Secure Shell, which gives you an encrypted remote terminal. EC2 uses key-based authentication. When you launched the instance, you chose a key pair, the public key was placed on the instance, and you kept the private key, usually a dot pem file. You connect by running ssh with the identity flag pointing at that file, then the username and the instance's public address. On the official Ubuntu images, the default user is ubuntu. Now, almost everyone's first attempt fails with a warning about an unprotected private key file. Don't panic. SSH deliberately refuses private keys that other users on your machine can read, because a readable private key isn't private any more. The fix is to restrict permissions with chmod 400, which makes the file readable by its owner only. I've seen people try chmod 777 out of sheer frustration, which does the exact opposite: everyone can read, write and execute it. Please don't. Treat the private key like a password. Don't email it, don't upload it, don't commit it, and lock port 22 down to your own IP in the security group, just like we said last week.",
