          heading: "Keeping the app running: PM2 and systemd",
          narration: "So you've connected, installed your app, and started it by typing node followed by your server file. It works! Until you close the SSH session, which can take the process down with it. Or the app crashes on an unhandled error. Or the server reboots after an update. Then the site's down, and nothing brings it back. I know this because one of my early side projects was down for an entire weekend: I'd started it by hand, then logged off for a nice relaxing Friday evening. Production apps run under a process manager. PM2 is a popular one for Node. It starts your app as a background process, restarts it automatically if it crashes, collects its output into log files, and, with its startup and save commands, brings your apps back when the server reboots. It can also run several instances to use multiple CPU cores. The alternative built into Ubuntu is systemd, the service manager that starts system services. You write a small unit file describing how to run your app, enable it, and systemd starts it at boot, restarts it on failure, and records logs you can read with journalctl. Either way, your app survives crashes, logouts and reboots.",
