          heading: "Infrastructure as code",
          narration: "So far we've created cloud resources by clicking through consoles. Fine for learning, but hard to repeat exactly, impossible to review, and very easy to forget what you changed. Ever tried to remember which box you ticked three weeks ago? Infrastructure as code means describing your servers, networks, databases and permissions in text files, which tools then use to create and update the real resources. On AWS, CloudFormation uses YAML or JSON templates, the AWS Cloud Development Kit lets you write the same definitions in TypeScript or other languages, and Terraform is a popular tool that works across many cloud providers. Azure has its own template languages too. Because the definitions live in Git, infrastructure changes get the same treatment as code: pull requests, reviews, history and rollback. You can spin up an identical staging environment from the same files, and tear everything down cleanly when a project ends, which, given last week's story about the holiday bill, is no small thing. Most of these tools are declarative. You describe the end state you want, and the tool works out what to create, change or delete to get there. It's like handing a builder the plans, instead of instructions for every brick.",
